## Interface: 20506
## Category: Commander
## Title: Commander Oracle
## Author: Devin
## Version: 1.8.0
## Notes: The battleground battle computer. In Arathi Basin the BATTLE tab answers the three questions the scoreboard cannot — who takes this from here and by how much, the LEAST you have to hold to win, and how many seconds you have left to take one more before nothing you do matters — by reading the five flags off the map's area POIs, the resources off the client's own widget, and running the race to 2000 forward at the server's real tick rates. In EVERY battleground the roster tabs read the server's own scoreboard, all forty players rather than the ones your combat log happened to see, and rank both sides by Impact, scored the way a StarCraft post-game screen reads a match rather than the way a raid meter does: output on their own currency of damage or healing, the TRADE they got for it (that output against the lives they spent buying it), killing blows, and objectives last and lightest -- a 0-to-5 base count saturates its own share instantly and is a terrible instrument for measuring a player, whatever it does to the match. Every part is a share of what the best three in THIS match are doing, with the raw figure and its per-minute rate on the tooltip. TOP names the best players on either side and the one line worth acting on — the healer to focus, the player carrying the enemy team; a tab per faction breaks that side down by killing blows, damage, healing and the map's own objective columns. The rankings carry a range column that says what KIND of answer it is — a measured distance for your own raid, an upper bound for anyone you are holding a unit for, and nothing at all for the rest, because that is all the client will say about an enemy. Every roster row IS the click target: left-click targets that player, right-click focuses them, on the same rows that carry the name, the score and the range. Targeting by name is a protected action, so a row's structure — which player is on it, where it sits — is settled out of combat and holds while you fight, while its paint (name, score, range, target highlight) is written every frame either way. What you are reading is always live, and what you click can never disagree with what you read, because the name is painted from the same assignment that set the macro. And when you hold the lead or an assist, Oracle will put raid icons on your own side's best players and keep them there as the match moves — off until you switch it on, sticky so nothing re-shuffles on a rounding difference, and careful never to remove an icon it did not place or take one that is already somebody else's. In an ARENA it marks a different question: your own team by CLASS COLOUR — the druid on the orange circle, the rogue on the yellow star, the priest on the skull — matched on hue rather than raw colour distance, because a pale cyan and a pale green are numerically close and perceptually nothing alike, and matched over the team actually present so two of the same class still get two different icons. With Commander Orders, click a flag on the board and the arrow points you at it.
